## 2. What the strongest existing methods already supply

The simplest direct routes were strong enough that the novelty claim needed narrowing.

Information–disturbance theory already implies that when the source forgets its input, its complete pure complement contains recoverable quantum information. That is the abstract reason transfer should be possible. It does not by itself construct a receiver that can perform the recovery while a field passes by. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}

Finite-memory compression of tensor-network families is also established. In particular, Bai, Yang and Chiribella give polynomial-time compression algorithms for matrix-product-state families. Arbitrary compression gates on individual temporal bins, however, do not automatically become bounded continuous-time couplings to a propagating field. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"}

The closest constructive precedent is Yang–Huelga–Plenio’s continuous quantum decoder. Their construction already supplies the central absorber mechanism for specified source states. Applying it to a virtual reference–source pair immediately suggests a receiver for an entire input channel. **Unknown-input universality alone is therefore not a defensible claim of a new absorber mechanism.** :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"}

Tsang’s general reversal theory provides another substantial precedent. Its reversal condition starts from a source–receiver steady-state purification; the paper explicitly leaves the conditions for convergence from other initial states outside its scope. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}

Purpose-designed transmission links are the engineering baseline when source control is available. Shaped cavity emission and absorption already provide ideal state-transfer constructions, and recent giant-atom architectures encode substantial control into the device geometry. I have not established a speed, energy or fault-tolerance advantage over those architectures. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}

The additional result developed here is the **complete finite-error construction**:

| Requirement | What the construction supplies |
|---|---|
| Unknown input, possibly entangled with a reference | A uniform channel-level error bound |
| Local receiver initialization | No initial source–receiver entanglement |
